      "H2: Key Design Rules for Maximum Scannability on Printed Signs",
      "Even the most accurately configured Google Maps QR code will fail if physical printing standards are ignored. Follow these expert design guidelines to guarantee 100% scan reliability in real-world environments:",
      "1. High Contrast Requirements: Always place dark QR modules against a light background (preferably crisp white). Avoid inverted designs (light modules on dark backgrounds) or low-contrast color combinations like grey on blue, as smartphone camera sensors struggle to parse them in direct sunlight or dim lighting.",
      "2. Minimum Physical Sizing: The minimum physical size for a scanned QR code depends on the viewing distance. For standard business cards or hand-held brochures, the code should be at least 2 cm x 2 cm (0.8 in x 0.8 in). For poster boards or storefront window decals scanned from 1 to 2 meters away, ensure the matrix is at least 10 cm x 10 cm (4 in x 4 in).",
      "3. Error Correction Level (Reed-Solomon H-Tier): If you plan to embed a central Google Maps icon or business logo inside your QR code, ensure your generator is configured to High (H) error correction. This reserves up to 30% of the internal matrix for redundant parity data, allowing decoders to parse the link even if a portion of the code is obscured by graphic logos or physical wear.",
      "4. Protective Quiet Zone Margins: Always maintain an unobstructed 'quiet zone' border around the outer edges of the QR matrix—equal to at least 4 module widths. Cutting off this border with graphic borders or text alignment confuses camera orientation algorithms.",
